WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.880
Welcome. This short tutorial brings
together practical examples of ideas and

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.408
tools for creating accessible
research and educational material.

3
00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:15.093
The aim is to make research and
teaching material easier to create,

4
00:00:15.093 --> 00:00:18.288
easier to understand,
and easier to revisit.

5
00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:21.849
The presentation also
demonstrates its own approach:

6
00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:25.985
the same slides and speaker notes
can guide a live presenter or,

7
00:00:25.985 --> 00:00:28.432
as here, an AI-generated tutorial.

8
00:00:29.282 --> 00:00:32.713
The first tool is a
LaTeX formula generator.

9
00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:38.353
Instead of remembering LaTeX syntax, you
describe the equation in ordinary language.

10
00:00:38.553 --> 00:00:42.791
Here, the prompt at the bottom left
asks for the integral of f of x,

11
00:00:42.791 --> 00:00:46.094
where f of x is x to the
fourth power plus three.

12
00:00:46.294 --> 00:00:50.178
The assistant inserts the matching
LaTeX code into the document,

13
00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:53.220
and you can choose to
keep or undo the change.

14
00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.037
On the right is the compiled result:

15
00:00:56.037 --> 00:01:00.325
f of x equals x to the fourth
plus three, and its integral,

16
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:03.991
x to the fifth over five,
plus three x, plus C.

17
00:01:04.191 --> 00:01:08.958
This example uses OpenAI's Prism,
linked at the bottom of the slide.

18
00:01:09.158 --> 00:01:12.071
Whatever the tool, always
check that the generated

19
00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:15.642
equation and notation match
the mathematics you intended.

20
00:01:16.492 --> 00:01:21.421
Next: creating videos with Claude,
anchored in presentation slides.

21
00:01:21.621 --> 00:01:25.729
Start with your own slides. They
define the content and the sequence,

22
00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:30.683
and act as the ground truth that keeps
the video focused on the subject matter.

23
00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:36.346
Writing your own narrative anchors the AI
agent and helps to avoid hallucinations.

24
00:01:36.546 --> 00:01:39.053
Speaker notes then
supply the explanations,

25
00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:42.334
for you as the presenter
and as guidance for the AI.

26
00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:45.811
With the slides and notes,
Claude can use narration and

27
00:01:45.811 --> 00:01:49.576
video tools to assemble a
complete tutorial, like this one.

28
00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:54.079
For the voice, you can choose an
open-source voice model, as in this video,

29
00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:58.919
or a service such as ElevenLabs for a
more natural voice with better intonation.

30
00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.442
You could even clone your own voice.

31
00:02:01.442 --> 00:02:04.918
That is optional, and a
question worth discussing.

32
00:02:05.118 --> 00:02:08.253
You can also ask for subtitles
in different languages,

33
00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:10.651
for more inclusion and accessibility.

34
00:02:10.851 --> 00:02:14.940
On the right are example videos
made this way, on AlphaFold 2,

35
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.949
diffusion, and tokenization,
available at the link below.

36
00:02:19.149 --> 00:02:23.788
Before publishing, review the narration,
the visuals, and the subtitles.

37
00:02:24.638 --> 00:02:29.079
The third idea: using video
transcripts for references.

38
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:31.966
Long recorded lectures,
like this one on machine

39
00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:34.318
learning for protein structure prediction,

40
00:02:34.318 --> 00:02:37.087
are hard to search by
scrubbing through them.

41
00:02:37.287 --> 00:02:40.451
The lecture is linked at
the top left of the slide.

42
00:02:40.651 --> 00:02:43.188
On YouTube, you can
open the transcript with

43
00:02:43.188 --> 00:02:45.917
the Show transcript
button below the video.

44
00:02:46.117 --> 00:02:50.825
It opens a panel where every line of
spoken text is linked to a timestamp.

45
00:02:51.025 --> 00:02:54.574
Give an AI assistant the video's
URL and ask it to read the

46
00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:58.304
transcript and return the timestamp
of a specific explanation.

47
00:02:58.304 --> 00:03:01.201
You can also supply the
transcript directly.

48
00:03:01.401 --> 00:03:03.973
Automatic transcripts can contain errors,

49
00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:09.117
so watch the actual passage and check
its context before citing or sharing it.

50
00:03:09.967 --> 00:03:13.259
The fourth idea is interactive graphs.

51
00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:16.679
When many curves share one
plot, as in the lower chart,

52
00:03:16.679 --> 00:03:19.154
following any single one is difficult.

53
00:03:19.354 --> 00:03:22.678
Both charts show ROC curves
per forecast lead time,

54
00:03:22.678 --> 00:03:25.481
comparing a neural
network with a baseline.

55
00:03:25.481 --> 00:03:28.284
In the upper chart,
one scenario stands out

56
00:03:28.284 --> 00:03:31.272
while the others fade into the background.

57
00:03:31.472 --> 00:03:35.129
That is because a forecast scenario
was selected in the legend,

58
00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:37.410
which makes its graph more salient.

59
00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.471
This is possible because Weights
and Biases supports Vega-Lite,

60
00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:44.908
a language for customizing
charts for accessibility.

61
00:03:45.108 --> 00:03:48.233
With it, you can adjust
colors, labels, and legends,

62
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:52.869
and add interactive highlighting when
hovering over or clicking on lines.

63
00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:56.357
Choices like these make
comparisons easier to follow.

64
00:03:56.357 --> 00:04:01.632
So aim for readable colors and clear
labels, as well as interactive highlighting.

65
00:04:02.482 --> 00:04:07.620
The last idea: intuitive
visualization and animation libraries.

66
00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.405
Visualization and animation
can make a mathematical

67
00:04:11.405 --> 00:04:14.397
process easier to follow, step by step.

68
00:04:14.597 --> 00:04:19.099
At the top is smalldiffusion, a
library focused on diffusion models.

69
00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:23.271
These panels show how its output
changes as the guidance scale,

70
00:04:23.271 --> 00:04:26.434
labelled CFG scale,
goes from zero to three.

71
00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:30.685
Below is Manim, a more general
mathematical animation engine,

72
00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:35.185
shown here with a curve, a secant
line, and the area beneath it.

73
00:04:35.385 --> 00:04:38.125
Because the animation
is generated from code,

74
00:04:38.125 --> 00:04:41.229
libraries like these help
reduce the hallucinations

75
00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:44.645
that often arise from purely
video-based generators.

76
00:04:44.845 --> 00:04:47.666
You can give them to an
AI agent as dependencies,

77
00:04:47.666 --> 00:04:50.621
so it can build the visual
explanation for you.

78
00:04:50.821 --> 00:04:53.665
As always, inspect the
code and check that the

79
00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:56.574
animation represents
the concept correctly.

80
00:04:56.774 --> 00:04:59.328
A good way to start is
with one concept that

81
00:04:59.328 --> 00:05:03.932
would benefit from a visual explanation.
Then review and refine the result.

82
00:05:04.782 --> 00:05:07.712
Finally, this slide collects
the links to the tools

83
00:05:07.712 --> 00:05:10.605
and examples used
throughout the presentation.

84
00:05:10.805 --> 00:05:14.051
Prism for LaTeX editing,
the video tutorial examples,

85
00:05:14.051 --> 00:05:19.263
Weights and Biases for experiment tracking,
and Vega-Lite for interactive graphs.

86
00:05:19.463 --> 00:05:22.935
Then smalldiffusion for
diffusion model visualizations,

87
00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:27.794
Manim for mathematical animations,
and the example video with timestamps.

88
00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:31.365
The project page gathers
everything in one place:

89
00:05:31.365 --> 00:05:34.255
the downloadable presentation, the poster,

90
00:05:34.255 --> 00:05:37.601
the video, voice samples,
and resource links.

91
00:05:38.451 --> 00:05:42.091
That's all for this overview.
Thank you for watching.
